1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:07,149
In 1990, the average Pole produced less than a third of what the average German did.

2
00:00:07,149 --> 00:00:09,125
Today, it's three quarters.

3
00:00:10,125 --> 00:00:14,127
On the way, Poland overtook Hungary, Greece and Portugal,

4
00:00:14,127 --> 00:00:17,075
and it's now within five percent of Spain.

5
00:00:18,075 --> 00:00:21,755
This is how it happened, in thirty-five years of numbers,

6
00:00:21,755 --> 00:00:23,950
and what those numbers don't show.

7
00:00:25,550 --> 00:00:29,146
First, what we're measuring. This is GDP per person:

8
00:00:29,146 --> 00:00:34,677
the value of everything a country produces in a year, divided by its population.

9
00:00:34,677 --> 00:00:37,374
It's in constant international dollars,

10
00:00:37,374 --> 00:00:44,150
which take out both inflation and the differences in what things cost from one country to another.

11
00:00:45,150 --> 00:00:49,890
It's the standard way to compare average living standards between countries.

12
00:00:49,890 --> 00:00:53,633
It isn't everything: it doesn't tell you who gets the money,

13
00:00:53,633 --> 00:00:57,500
or how many hours people work for it. We'll come back to that.

14
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:03,450
We're following ten countries: the four biggest economies of western Europe,

15
00:01:03,450 --> 00:01:08,725
three from the south, and three from central Europe: Czechia, Hungary and Poland.

16
00:01:10,125 --> 00:01:16,381
1990. Communism in Poland has just ended. Poland's figure is 12,

17
00:01:16,381 --> 00:01:20,975
821 dollars a person, the lowest on this chart.

18
00:01:21,975 --> 00:01:32,109
Hungary is at 21,297. Greece and Portugal, at about 27,000. And Germany, at 42,355:

19
00:01:32,109 --> 00:01:35,650
more than three times Poland.

20
00:01:36,650 --> 00:01:42,402
On the first of January 1990, Poland started a fast and painful reform programme,

21
00:01:42,402 --> 00:01:46,875
often called shock therapy. Prices were freed almost overnight,

22
00:01:46,875 --> 00:01:51,775
support for state firms was cut, and the currency became convertible.

23
00:01:52,775 --> 00:01:58,398
The first years hurt. In 1991, the figure fell by seven percent,

24
00:01:58,398 --> 00:02:05,250
and unemployment rose fast. Poland only got back above its 1990 level in 1994.

25
00:02:06,450 --> 00:02:10,000
But then the economy grew, and it kept on growing.

26
00:02:12,400 --> 00:02:18,700
By 2000, Poland is at 18,566 dollars. Still the lowest of the ten,

27
00:02:18,700 --> 00:02:22,900
but its gap to Hungary has more than halved.

28
00:02:24,300 --> 00:02:30,864
2004. Poland joins the European Union, together with nine other countries,

29
00:02:30,864 --> 00:02:37,250
including Czechia and Hungary. Poland's figure: 20,879 dollars a person.

30
00:02:38,250 --> 00:02:40,827
Membership brought three big things.

31
00:02:40,827 --> 00:02:44,836
Polish firms could sell freely across the single market.

32
00:02:44,836 --> 00:02:50,492
Western companies built factories and offices. And EU funds paid for new roads,

33
00:02:50,492 --> 00:02:52,425
railways and water systems.

34
00:02:53,425 --> 00:02:55,960
It also opened western job markets.

35
00:02:55,960 --> 00:02:59,944
Hundreds of thousands of Poles moved to work in the UK,

36
00:02:59,944 --> 00:03:04,725
in Ireland and later in Germany, and many of them sent money home.

37
00:03:05,725 --> 00:03:13,033
From 2004 to 2008, Poland's figure rose by 22 percent, the fastest of the ten.

38
00:03:13,033 --> 00:03:15,375
Czechia was close behind.

39
00:03:16,775 --> 00:03:22,630
Then the financial crisis hits Europe. Watch Greece: 41,

40
00:03:22,630 --> 00:03:29,950
709 dollars a person in 2008, down to 37,567 by 2010. Spain falls too,

41
00:03:29,950 --> 00:03:33,400
and so does almost everyone else.

42
00:03:34,400 --> 00:03:40,160
Poland doesn't fall. In 2009, its economy grew by two point six percent.

43
00:03:40,160 --> 00:03:44,400
Every other country in today's European Union shrank.

44
00:03:45,400 --> 00:03:48,356
Economists usually point to a few reasons:

45
00:03:48,356 --> 00:03:51,805
a large home market that depends less on exports,

46
00:03:51,805 --> 00:03:55,887
a currency that fell and made Polish goods cheaper abroad,

47
00:03:55,887 --> 00:03:58,350
and EU money that kept on arriving.

48
00:03:59,750 --> 00:04:04,725
And now, the crossings. In 2011, Poland passes Hungary.

49
00:04:05,925 --> 00:04:10,651
Greece's debt crisis keeps pulling its line down. By 2013,

50
00:04:10,651 --> 00:04:14,400
it has lost about a quarter of its 2008 level.

51
00:04:15,200 --> 00:04:18,425
In 2015, Poland passes Greece.

52
00:04:19,625 --> 00:04:24,375
Then Portugal. Through the late 2010s, the gap between them halves.

53
00:04:25,175 --> 00:04:30,389
In 2020, the pandemic year, Portugal's figure falls by eight percent.

54
00:04:30,389 --> 00:04:35,150
Poland's dips by less than one percent, and Poland moves ahead.

55
00:04:36,550 --> 00:04:40,319
After the pandemic, Poland bounces straight back:

56
00:04:40,319 --> 00:04:43,550
up eight and a half percent in 2021 alone.

57
00:04:44,550 --> 00:04:48,877
2025. Poland: 46,925 dollars a person.

58
00:04:48,877 --> 00:04:56,050
That's three point seven times its 1990 level, after inflation.

59
00:04:57,050 --> 00:05:07,630
Spain: 49,318. Czechia, almost exactly the same. Italy and the UK, about 54,000.

60
00:05:07,630 --> 00:05:10,275
And Germany, 62,824.

61
00:05:11,275 --> 00:05:16,803
Poland has gone from 30 percent of Germany's level to 75 percent,

62
00:05:16,803 --> 00:05:19,950
and from 40 percent of Spain's to 95.

63
00:05:21,550 --> 00:05:25,975
But this line doesn't show everything. Here are three things it misses.

64
00:05:26,975 --> 00:05:34,599
First, hours. In 2023, the average Polish worker worked about 1,807 hours.

65
00:05:34,599 --> 00:05:40,266
The average German worked 1,335. That's 472 hours more:

66
00:05:40,266 --> 00:05:43,975
about twelve extra forty-hour weeks.

67
00:05:44,975 --> 00:05:48,600
Of these ten countries, only Greece works longer.

68
00:05:49,600 --> 00:05:52,556
So per hour, the gap is much wider.

69
00:05:52,556 --> 00:05:59,060
A German worker produces about 82 dollars of output an hour. A Polish worker,

70
00:05:59,060 --> 00:06:05,141
about 47. Per person, Poland was at about 70 percent of Germany in 2023.

71
00:06:05,141 --> 00:06:07,675
Per hour worked, it was at 57.

72
00:06:09,275 --> 00:06:14,002
Second, not all of Poland is catching up at the same speed.

73
00:06:14,002 --> 00:06:20,572
Eurostat compares regions with the EU average, per person and adjusted for prices.

74
00:06:20,572 --> 00:06:26,100
In 2023, the region around Warsaw was at 155 percent of that average.

75
00:06:27,100 --> 00:06:34,352
Lubelskie, in the east, was at 53 percent. In 2004, the two were at 108 and 37.

76
00:06:34,352 --> 00:06:39,125
Both have risen, but the gap between them has grown.

77
00:06:40,525 --> 00:06:47,251
Third, people. Poland's population has stayed at about 38 million since 1990.

78
00:06:47,251 --> 00:06:53,454
The UN's central projection has it falling to about 33 million by 2050,

79
00:06:53,454 --> 00:06:55,725
and to 19 million by 2100.

80
00:06:56,725 --> 00:06:58,826
Fewer workers make growth harder.

81
00:06:58,826 --> 00:07:02,772
More of it will have to come from producing more in each hour,

82
00:07:02,772 --> 00:07:04,300
not from working longer.

83
00:07:05,900 --> 00:07:08,727
So, what's next? Over the last ten years,

84
00:07:08,727 --> 00:07:12,173
Poland's figure grew by about four percent a year.

85
00:07:12,173 --> 00:07:15,000
Spain's grew by less than one and a half.

86
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:18,885
If both kept that pace, and that's a big if,

87
00:07:18,885 --> 00:07:23,541
Poland would pass Spain in about two years. That's a simple projection,

88
00:07:23,541 --> 00:07:24,525
not a forecast.

89
00:07:25,525 --> 00:07:26,881
In thirty-five years,

90
00:07:26,881 --> 00:07:31,980
Poland went from the bottom of this chart to within reach of Spain and Czechia.

91
00:07:31,980 --> 00:07:34,950
None of the other nine came close to its pace.

92
00:07:36,550 --> 00:07:43,619
Three things to take away. One: Poland's GDP per person, after inflation and prices,

93
00:07:43,619 --> 00:07:47,575
is three point seven times what it was in 1990.

94
00:07:48,575 --> 00:07:54,819
Two: it passed Hungary in 2011, Greece in 2015 and Portugal in 2020,

95
00:07:54,819 --> 00:07:58,125
and it's now at 95 percent of Spain.

96
00:07:59,125 --> 00:08:03,153
Three: the catch-up isn't finished. Poles work long hours,

97
00:08:03,153 --> 00:08:08,500
Warsaw has pulled far ahead of the east, and the population is set to shrink.

98
00:08:09,700 --> 00:08:15,900
Next on Terrachart: how long Europeans live, and how the south overtook the north.

99
00:08:17,100 --> 00:08:21,085
Sources are in the description. If you live in Poland, tell us:

100
00:08:21,085 --> 00:08:25,575
does this match what you've seen where you live? We read every comment.
